{"name":"Royal Orchard","hex":"#5f6d59","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"royal","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or relating to a monarch or his (or her) family.","Having the air or demeanour of a monarch; illustrious; magnanimous; of more than common size or excellence."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A royal person; a member of a royal family.","A standard size of printing paper, measuring 25 by 20 inches."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/royal"},{"term":"orchard","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A garden or an area of land for the cultivation of fruit or nut trees.","The trees themselves cultivated in such an area."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/orchard"}],"entries":[{"title":"Orchard","blocks":[{"text":"An orchard is an intentional plantation of trees or shrubs that is maintained for food production. Orchards comprise fruit- or nut-producing trees that are generally grown for commercial production. Orchards are also sometimes a feature of large gardens, where they serve an aesthetic as well as a productive purpose. A fruit garden is generally synonymous with an orchard, although it is set on a smaller, non-commercial scale and may emphasize berry shrubs in preference to fruit trees. Most temperate-zone orchards are laid out in a regular grid, with a grazed or mown grass or bare soil base that makes maintenance and fruit gathering easy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Orchard_Lanes.jpg/330px-Orchard_Lanes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchard"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Streuobstwiesen%2C_Blick_auf_Traufgang_und_Lochenh%C3%B6rnle.jpg/500px-Streuobstwiesen%2C_Blick_auf_Traufgang_und_Lochenh%C3%B6rnle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.